---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';

const MACHINES = [
  { name: 'ZX Spectrum', net: 'Next WiFi / Spectranet', repo: 'rachel-sinclair-zx-spectrum', evidence: 'Build + protocol', hardware: 'Real adapter path' },
  { name: 'Commodore 64', net: 'Ultimate-II / Hayes modem', repo: 'rachel-c64', evidence: 'Build + protocol', hardware: 'Real adapter path' },
  { name: 'Commodore VIC-20', net: 'Hayes user-port modem', repo: 'rachel-vic20', evidence: 'CI artifact', hardware: 'Real adapter path' },
  { name: 'Commodore Amiga', net: 'Hayes serial modem', repo: 'rachel-amiga', evidence: 'Build + protocol', hardware: 'Real adapter path' },
  { name: 'BBC Micro', net: 'User-port WiFi bridge', repo: 'rachel-bbc-micro', evidence: 'CI artifact', hardware: 'Real adapter path' },
  { name: 'Acorn Electron', net: 'WiFi expansion', repo: 'rachel-electron', evidence: 'CI artifact', hardware: 'Real adapter path' },
  { name: 'Atari 8-bit', net: 'FujiNet', repo: 'rachel-atari8', evidence: 'CI artifact', hardware: 'Real adapter path' },
  { name: 'Atari 7800', net: 'Cartridge coprocessor', repo: 'rachel-atari-7800', evidence: 'Build only', hardware: 'Design needed' },
  { name: 'Oric Atmos', net: '$031C 6551 + Hayes modem', repo: 'rachel-oric', evidence: 'CI transport build', hardware: 'Prototype needed' },
  { name: 'Dragon 32 / CoCo', net: 'Serial / DragonWiFi', repo: 'rachel-dragon', evidence: 'Build + protocol', hardware: 'Model dependent' },
  { name: 'MSX', net: 'GR8NET TCP/IP UNAPI', repo: 'rachel-msx', evidence: 'Emulator protocol', hardware: 'Real adapter path' },
  { name: 'ColecoVision', net: '$50–$52 expansion FIFO', repo: 'rachel-coleco-colecovision', evidence: 'Build only', hardware: 'Prototype needed' },
  { name: 'Sega Master System', net: 'Cartridge adapter', repo: 'rachel-sega-mastersystem', evidence: 'CI artifact', hardware: 'Prototype needed' },
  { name: 'Sega Game Gear', net: 'Cartridge adapter', repo: 'rachel-sega-gamegear', evidence: 'CI artifact', hardware: 'Prototype needed' },
  { name: 'Nintendo NES', net: 'Controller-port bridge', repo: 'rachel-nintendo-nes', evidence: 'Emulator protocol', hardware: 'Prototype needed' },
  { name: 'Nintendo Game Boy', net: 'Buffered link bridge', repo: 'rachel-nintendo-gameboy', evidence: 'CI artifact', hardware: 'Prototype needed' },
];

const ORG = 'https://github.com/rachel-multiverse';
const TEST_REPORT = `${ORG}/protocol/issues/new?template=hardware-validation.yml`;
---

<Layout
  title="Ports - Rachel"
  description="Rachel is being written for sixteen vintage machines in Z80, 6502, 6809 and 68000 assembly, all speaking the same 64-byte protocol as the iOS app."
>
  <main class="ports">
    <div class="container">
      <h1>One card game, every machine</h1>

      <p class="lede">
        Sixteen machines, in Z80, 6502, 6809 and 68000 assembly, all speaking
        the same <a href="/protocol">64-byte protocol</a> as the iPhone app.
        Every one of them is a public repository you can read today.
      </p>

      <p class="honest">
        <strong>No vintage client is released or hardware-verified yet.</strong>
        CI artifacts are test builds, not releases. “Emulator protocol” proves
        message compatibility, not the screen, controls, adapter, or a physical
        machine. The labels below say what evidence actually exists.
      </p>

      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <caption>Software evidence and physical-network status for each vintage Rachel client</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Machine</th>
              <th>Networking</th>
              <th>Evidence</th>
              <th>Hardware</th>
              <th>Links</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {MACHINES.map((m) => (
              <tr>
                <td class="machine">{m.name}</td>
                <td class="net">{m.net}</td>
                <td><span class="badge evidence">{m.evidence}</span></td>
                <td><span class="badge hardware">{m.hardware}</span></td>
                <td class="links">
                  <a href={`${ORG}/${m.repo}`}>source</a>
                  <a href={`${ORG}/${m.repo}/actions`}>builds</a>
                </td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <section>
        <h2>How a 1982 machine keeps up</h2>
        <p>
          It doesn't run the game. The host does — it owns the deck, the rules
          and the shuffle — and each client renders what it is told and sends
          back what its player pressed. So a machine measured in kilobytes can
          sit at the same table as an iPad: it needs enough memory to draw a
          hand of cards, not enough to referee a game.
        </p>
        <p>
          That is also what makes <em>same rules everywhere</em> true rather
          than merely claimed. Sixteen separate implementations of stacking and
          compulsory counters would each have to agree exactly, and the first
          time they didn't, a game between two particular machines would come
          apart. One engine and many renderers makes that impossible.
        </p>
        <p>
          The trade is honest and worth knowing: a vintage machine with no host
          on the network has no game to play. Solo play is a capability of
          platforms that also implement the rules locally, which today means
          the iOS app.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>What the labels mean</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>CI artifact</strong> means GitHub assembled a downloadable
          test image. <strong>Build + protocol</strong> adds fixture or harness
          checks. <strong>Emulator protocol</strong> executes those checks in
          Emu198x. Hardware labels are separate on purpose: a green software
          build does not prove voltage levels, timing, cabling, modem firmware,
          controls, or a complete game on the original machine.
        </p>
        <p class="tester-cta">
          Own one of these machines or adapters?
          <a href={TEST_REPORT}>Run the five-minute smoke test and report it</a>.
          The form asks for reproducible, privacy-safe evidence and credits the
          exact hardware, firmware, client, and server revisions.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Writing one yourself</h2>
        <p>
          The <a href="/protocol">specification</a> is published in full, in
          <a href="https://github.com/rachel-multiverse/protocol">its own
          repository</a> along with the shared conformance fixtures — the
          message vectors a client checks itself against, so a new port can
          prove itself against the format before it ever reaches a real game.
        </p>
        <p>
          Build pages linked above contain the current test artifacts where a
          repository uploads them. They expire with GitHub's artifact retention
          window; durable downloads will arrive with actual releases.
        </p>
        <p>
          If a machine you love is missing, it is missing because nobody has
          started it.
        </p>
      </section>

      <p class="back-link"><a href="/">&lsaquo; Back to Rachel</a></p>
    </div>
  </main>
</Layout>

<style>
  .ports {
    background: var(--felt-deep);
    padding: 4rem 0;
  }

  .container {
    max-width: 820px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 0 1.5rem;
  }

  h1 {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 2.75rem);
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
    line-height: 1.1;
  }

  .lede {
    font-size: 1.0625rem;
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
  }

  .honest {
    padding: 1rem 1.25rem;
    background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.045);
    border-left: 3px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.25);
    border-radius: 0.4rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    margin-bottom: 2.5rem;
  }

  .honest strong { color: var(--text); }

  /* The table is four columns of short values; on a phone it still needs to
     scroll rather than force the page sideways. */
  .table-wrap {
    overflow-x: auto;
    margin-bottom: 3rem;
  }

  table {
    width: 100%;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
  }

  caption {
    position: absolute;
    width: 1px;
    height: 1px;
    padding: 0;
    margin: -1px;
    overflow: hidden;
    clip: rect(0, 0, 0, 0);
    white-space: nowrap;
    border: 0;
  }

  th, td {
    text-align: left;
    padding: 0.6rem 0.75rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.1);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  th { color: var(--text); font-weight: 600; }
  td { color: var(--text-muted); }

  .machine { color: var(--text); }

  .net { font-size: 0.875rem; }

  .badge {
    display: inline-block;
    padding: 0.18rem 0.5rem;
    border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.16);
    border-radius: 999px;
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    line-height: 1.35;
  }

  .evidence { color: var(--text); background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.06); }
  .hardware { color: var(--text-muted); }

  .links {
    display: flex;
    gap: 0.7rem;
  }

  .links a {
    min-height: 44px;
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    touch-action: manipulation;
  }

  .tester-cta {
    padding: 1rem 1.25rem;
    border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.13);
    border-radius: 0.4rem;
  }

  code {
    font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, monospace;
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    background: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.3);
    padding: 0.15em 0.45em;
    border-radius: 0.25rem;
  }

  section { margin-bottom: 2.5rem; }

  h2 {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 1.4rem;
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
  }

  p { color: var(--text-muted); margin-bottom: 1rem; }

  .back-link {
    margin-top: 3rem;
    padding-top: 2rem;
    border-top: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.1);
  }
</style>
